Looking at the graph, the curve crosses the x-axis at four points: approximately x = -3, x = -1, x = 1, and x = 3. Between these crossings, the function dips below the axis (minima near x = -2.2 and x = 2.2) and rises above it (local maximum at x = 0, y = 1). Outside these points, the function grows upward and stays positive within [-6, 6].

Each crossing is a simple sign change, giving distinct real zeros at x ≈ -3, -1, 1, 3 — all within the interval [-6, 6].

\boxed{4}
